  "items": [
    {
      "id": "a01",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 2,
      "estimated_minutes": 2,
      "reason": "The reviewer need only distinguish the description’s disputed-coverage settlements and allocation under subsections (b) and (c) from the court’s interest-classification and direct-liability disputes. Although it quotes “within applicable policy limits,” its stated problem is explicit and requires no factual investigation."
    },
    {
      "id": "a02",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 3,
      "estimated_minutes": 3,
      "reason": "One additional look separates the description’s concern about whether an excess form contains supplementary-payment provisions from d03’s question about which policies the reference encompasses. The additional concern about unallocated or in-house costs is plainly identifiable as a separate issue."
    },
    {
      "id": "a03",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 2,
      "estimated_minutes": 2,
      "reason": "The description specifically targets “such settlement or appeal” in the appellate proviso, allowing a quick comparison with the court’s primary-versus-excess scope dispute and its self-insured-retention explanation for proportionality. No settlement history or allocation calculation is needed to identify the issue it raises."
    },
    {
      "id": "a04",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 1,
      "estimated_minutes": 1,
      "reason": "The description expressly challenges the subjective exposure trigger, “in the judgment of the CORPORATION.” Comparing that objection with the court’s construction of the capacity in which the “REINSURED” acts is a single, clear-cut step."
    },
    {
      "id": "a05",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 3,
      "estimated_minutes": 3,
      "reason": "The reviewer takes a brief look at the adjacent supplementary-payment and assumption-of-defense provisions to identify the asserted conflict concerning partially covered defenses. The comparison then turns plainly on that coordination issue versus d07’s treatment of statutory prejudgment interest under the exclusion."
    },
    {
      "id": "a06",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 2,
      "estimated_minutes": 2,
      "reason": "The description clearly identifies payment timing under “actually paid or payable” and the Loss Payable condition. The reviewer can distinguish that issue from the court’s construction of “interest on any judgment” without resolving when indemnification becomes payable."
    }
  ]
}
